### QUESTION: MULTIPLE INSTANCES KE BEECH LOCAL LOCK USE KARNE SE KYA PROBLEM HAI?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- Agar Service horizontally scaled hai — matlab multiple instances run kar rahi hai, aur in instances ke
  beech requests distribute karne ke liye ek Service Load Balancer configured hai — to is case me local lock 
  kaam nahi karega. Kyunki har instance apna local lock independently acquire karti hai, aur local lock sirf
  same application instance ke threads ko coordinate karta hai — doosre instances ke threads ko nahi.

- Distributed system me hume aisa lock chahiye jo sabhi service instances ke beech shared ho. 
  Is coordination ke liye commonly Redis-based Distributed Lock use kiya ja sakta hai.

- Goal: Sirf ek instance → DB hit kare → Cache populate kare → Baaki instances wait karein



### QUESTION : REDIS KYUN ?
-----------------------------

Redis already sabhi Service instances ke liye shared cache ki tarah kaam kar raha hai. Isliye Redis me 
ek shared lock maintain karke multiple service instances ke beech coordination kiya ja sakta hai.

                  

### QUESTION : REDIS DISTRIBUTED LOCK KYA HAI AND KAISE CREATE HOTA HAI ? 
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Redis lock basically ek normal Redis key hoti hai.

Suppose GET /products/101 ke liye application ne Cache Key banayi hai:

    Cache Key → product:101

Agar isi cached data ke liye lock chahiye, to application ek naming
convention ke according corresponding Lock Key bana sakti hai:

    Lock Key  → lock:product:101


Note:
    lock: koi Redis ka special keyword nahi hai — ye sirf ek
    naming convention hai, jisse identify kiya ja sake ki:

    product:101       → Cached Data ki key
    lock:product:101  → is cached data ke liye Lock key

Redis ke liye dono normal keys hi hain.



### QUESTION: CACHE STAMPEDE SCENARIO ME CACHE MISS HONE KE BAAD REDIS LOCK ACQUISITION KAISE HOTA HAI?
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

a) Maan lo same product (product_id = 101) ke liye read requests teen different service instances par ek saath aa jaati hain.

b) Teeno instances is requested product ke liye same Cache Key create karti hain — say product:101. Ab sabhi instances Redis
   me is key ko check karti hain, aur teeno ko Cache Miss milta hai.

c) Ab teeno instances isi cache entry ke corresponding lock acquire karne ke liye application me defined naming convention ke
   according Lock Key derive karti hain — say lock:product:101

d) Ab teeno requests (hence 3 different threads, across 3 different instances) Redis me us cache entry par lock acquire karne ki
   koshish karti hain. Inme se sirf 1 thread ko hi is cache entry (product:101) par lock milega:

      Request 1 : SET lock:product:101 <R1-123> NX EX 10
      Request 2 : SET lock:product:101 <R2-456> NX EX 10
      Request 3 : SET lock:product:101 <R3-789> NX EX 10

      - lock:product:101
         → Ye Redis key hai jo lock represent karti hai, product:101 cache entry ke liye.

      - <owner-id>
         → Ye represent karta hai ki lock kis request ne acquire kiya hai. 

      - NX
         → Us cache entry ke liye lock tabhi create karo jab wo pehle se exist na kare.
           Agar lock key already exist kar rahi hai, iska matlab kisi doosri request ne
           us cache entry par already lock le rakha hai — isliye nayi request ko lock nahi milega.

      - EX 10
         → Lock ke saath TTL = 10 seconds configured hai. Agar lock manually release na bhi ho,
           to Redis TTL expire hone ke baad automatically is lock key ko delete kar dega.
           Isse us cache entry ke liye lock automatically release ho jayega.
         → KYUN ZAROORI HAI ? : Maan lo kisi request (jo kisi service instance par aayi thi) ne lock acquire kiya
           aur wo DB se data fetch kar rahi hai but meanwhile wo instance crash ho gaya. Us case me agar TTL na ho,
           to us cache entry ke liye lock hamesha ke liye Redis me pada rahega, aur baaki requests jo us lock ka wait 
           kar rahi hain, wo indefinitely wait karti reh jaayengi. TTL is situation ko prevent karta hai.


e) Ab maan lo ki Request 1 (Instance 1 se) Redis tak sabse pehle pahunchti hai. Abhi Redis me lock key
   (lock:product:101) exist nahi karti — isliye Redis successfully lock key create kar deta hai. Hence
   Request 1 ne us cache entry (product:101) par lock acquire kar liya.

   Redis me lock aisa dikhega:

        lock:product:101  →  R1-abc  (owner-id)
        TTL = 10 seconds

f) Request 2 aur Request 3 bhi same lock acquire karne ki koshish karti hain — lekin NX ka rule hai:
   lock key tabhi create karo jab wo already exist na kare. Kyunki lock:product:101 ab already exist
   karti hai:

   Conceptually:

                       ┌── Request1 → Lock Acquired ✅
     Cache Miss ───────┤
     (product:101)     ├── Request2 → Lock Acquisition Failed ❌
                       │
                       └── Request3 → Lock Acquisition Failed ❌

   

g) Request 2 aur Request 3 ko lock nahi mila. Ab Application ko decide karna hota hai ki lock acquisition failure ke baad kya strategy use karni hai.
   Common flow:
    -  Agar lock acquire ho gaya → Application DB se data fetch karti hai.
    -  Agar lock acquisition fail ho gaya → Application thodi der wait karti hai, phir Cache dobara check karti hai. 
        * Agar data ab available hai (kyunki doosri request ne DB se fetch karke Cache populate kar diya) → return data.
        * Agar ab bhi available nahi hai → Retry with exponential backoff, ya configured max retries ke baad error return.

h) Ab Request 1 ko lock mil gaya hai, so wo Database se data fetch karti hai (product:101) aur Redis me populate karti hai (product:101 → Product Data).
  
i) Ab Cache populate ho chuki hai. Request 2 aur Request 3 jo wait/retry kar rahi thi, wo Cache ko dobara check karti hain same key (product:101) ke liye
   aur ab unhe Cache Hit milega. Wo data directly Cache se return kar dengi client ko, Database ko dobara hit karne ki zarurat nahi padegi.

j) Finally, Request 1 lock release karti hai by deleting the lock key:
     DEL lock:product:101






2. STALE-WHILE-REVALIDATE 
----------------------------

- Ye bhi ek approach hai jo popular cache entry expire hone ke baad Cache Stampede prevent karne ke liye use ki jaati hai.

- Normal caching me har cache key ke saath ek TTL associated hota hai. TTL expire hone ke baad key Redis se remove ho jaati hai. 
  Agar usi waqt us data ke liye bahut saari concurrent requests aa jaayein, to sabko Cache Miss milta hai aur sab DB ko hit karne 
  lagti hain → Cache Stampede.

- Stale-While-Revalidate approach me hum cache entry ki lifetime ko logically do periods me divide karte hain — Fresh Time aur Stale Time. 
  Conceptually: 
   Redis TTL = Fresh Time + Stale Time

- Redis ko sirf key ke TTL ke baare me pata hota hai — na Fresh Time ka, na Stale Time ka. Redis ke perspective se key simply poore TTL tak 
  exist karegi, phir expire ho jaayegi. Freshness track karne ki responsibility Application ki hai. Iske liye Application key ki corresponding 
  value me ek extra attribute maintain karti hai — freshUntil — jo batata hai ki data kis time tak fresh maana jaayega. Uske baad data ko stale
  maana jaayega. 

- Stale Time ke dauran data Application ke perspective se technically stale hota hai — lekin Redis ke perspective se ye Cache Hit hai,
  kyunki Redis ke liye ye ek normal cache entry hai jiska TTL abhi expire nahi hua. Redis ko ye nahi pata ki Application is data ko "stale" maan 
  rahi hai — wo sirf itna jaanta hai ki key exist karti hai. Isliye Application ko stale data Redis se mil jaata hai, aur wo usse temporarily client
  ko serve kar deti hai — jab tak background me refresh complete nahi ho jaata.

  Application request pe check karti hai:

    * Current Time < freshUntil  →  Fresh Data
    * Current Time > freshUntil  →  Stale Data

- Example:
  
  Database me product:101 ka price = ₹79,999 hai. Isse Redis me cache karte hain, aur decide karte hain:

  Fresh Time = 10 minutes
  Stale Time = 5 minutes

  Redis TTL = Fresh + Stale = 15 minutes

  Redis me entry aisi dikhegi:

    Key:    product:101
    Value:  {
              "data": { "id": 101, "price": 79999 },
              "freshUntil": "10:10 AM"
            }
   TTL:    15 minutes


   Timeline (suppose data 10:00 AM pe Redis me store hua):

    10:00 → 10:10   →  Fresh Data
    10:10 → 10:15   →  Stale Data (Redis me available, temporarily serve allowed)
    After 10:15     →  Redis TTL expire → Cache key removed


- REQUEST FLOW :

  CASE 1: REQUEST FRESH TIME KE AANDAR AAYI
  -------------------------------------------

   Application ne Redis mein lookup kiya aur Cache Hit mila. Phir check kiya ki Current Time < freshUntil, yani data fresh hai. 
   Isliye application ne Redis se fresh data immediately client ko return kar diya, bina kisi DB call ke.

  CASE 2: REQUEST STALE TIME KE AANDAR AAYI
  -------------------------------------------

   a) Suppose bahut saari concurrent requests (hence different threads, spanned across different instances of the service) Stale Time me aa gayi.
     Is case me, Application ne har request ke liye Redis lookup kiya aur Cache Hit mila. Lekin check karne par pata chala ki Current Time > freshUntil 
     yaani data stale hai. 

   b) Ab saari concurrent requests us cache entry ko refresh karne ke liye 
      (i.e. DB se fetch karke cache ko update karna us entry ke liye) Redis par 
      lock acquire karne ki koshish karti hain. Inme se sirf 1 thread ko hi is 
      cache entry (say product:101) par lock milega:

       Cache Key: product:101
       Lock Key:  lock:refresh:product:101

       Request 1   : SET lock:refresh:product:101 <R1-123>   NX EX 10
       Request 2   : SET lock:refresh:product:101 <R2-456>   NX EX 10
       .
       .
       Request 100 : SET lock:refresh:product:101 <R100-789> NX EX 10

        NX ka rule: Lock key tabhi create karo jab wo us cache entry ke liye pehle 
        se exist na kare. Isliye sirf ek request lock acquire kar payegi. Baaki 
        sabko Lock Failed milega.

       Conceptually:

                               ┌── R1  → Lock Acquired ✅
        Stale Data Encounter ──┤
                               ├── R2  → Lock Failed ❌
                               ├── R3  → Lock Failed ❌
                               └── ... R10,000 → Lock Failed ❌


  c) Ab maan lo ki Request 1 (Instance 1 se) Redis tak sabse pehle pahunchti hai. Abhi Redis me lock key (lock:refresh:product:101) exist nahi karti 
     isliye Redis successfully lock key create kar deta hai. Hence Request 1 ne us cache entry (product:101) par lock acquire kar liya.

  d) Request 1 do kaam karti hai — pehla, wo async/background job trigger karti hai ("DB se latest data fetch karke Cache refresh karo"), aur doosra, wo 
     khud bhi stale data immediately client ko return kar deti hai. Iske saath hi lock ki ownership background worker ko transfer ho jaati hai, kyunki refresh 
     ka actual kaam wahi karega.

  e) Baaki requests jinko lock nahi mila, wo bhi stale data immediately client ko return kar deti hain — bas refresh job trigger nahi karti, kyunki wo kaam already 
     Request 1 (background worker ke through) kar rahi hai. Aur jab tak background refresh complete nahi hota, meanwhile aane wali new requests bhi Redis se same 
     stale data receive karti hain — wait nahi karti.

  f) Background worker DB se latest data fetch karta hai aur Cache me us key ke corresponding existing stale data ko latest data se replace kar deta hai. 
     Saath hi freshUntil update karta hai (naya freshUntil = current time + Fresh Time) aur Redis TTL reset karta hai (Fresh + Stale). 
     Jab refresh successfully complete ho jaata hai, tab background worker refresh lock release kar deta hai — DEL lock:refresh:product:101 — aur lock free ho jaata hai.



 ### QUESTION: REQUEST COALESCING AUR STALE-WHILE-REVALIDATE APPROACH ME KYA DIFFERENCE HAI ?
 ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 Jin requests ko particular cache entry ke liye Redis lock nahi milta, unka behavior dono approaches me alag hota hai:

 1: Normal Locking (Request Coalescing):
     Lock na milne par request wait/retry karti hai, aur phir Cache dobara check karti hai 
     jab tak fresh data Cache me available na ho jaaye. Is duration me us request ka thread 
     lock ka wait karte hue blocked rehta hai.

 2: Stale-While-Revalidate: 
    Lock na milne par request wait nahi karti already available stale data immediately return kar deti hai, 
    jisse user experience improve hota hai aur response time reduce hota hai. Request ka thread turant free 
    ho jaata hai — koi blocking wait nahi.



 ### QUESTION: AGAR REFRESH PROCESS BAHUT SLOW HO JAYE TO KYA HOGA ?
 --------------------------------------------------------------------
  Agar background refresh slow ho jaaye, to jab tak Redis key stale period ke andar available hai, incoming requests stale data serve karti rehti hain.
  Lekin agar refresh complete hone se pehle Redis TTL expire ho gaya, to cache entry remove ho jaayegi — aur usi time bahut saari concurrent requests 
  aa gayi, to Cache Stampede ka risk dobara aa sakta hai. Isliye Stale Time ko sufficiently large rakhna important hai, taaki background refresh ideally
  stale period ke andar hi complete ho jaaye.


 ### QUESTION: STALE-WHILE-REVALIDATE KAB USEFUL HOTA HAI?
 -----------------------------------------------------------

 Stale-While-Revalidate especially useful hota hai jab:

 - Slightly stale data temporarily acceptable ho.
 - Cached data derived/aggregated ho — aur har underlying data change par Cache Invalidation karna unnecessarily expensive ho.

  #### EXAMPLE: TRENDING PRODUCTS LIST
  -------------------------------------

   Trending Products List directly Database ki single row se nahi aati wo multiple tables/data sources se combine/calculate karke banayi jaati hai:

   Product Views + Purchases + Likes + Recent Activity
                    ↓
              Trending Score
                    ↓
            Trending Products List

   Underlying data continuously aur rapidly change hota hai — har view/purchase/like ke baad. Trending Products Cache ko invalidate karna unnecessarily expensive hai,
   kyunki bahut frequently invalidate karna padta hai. Saath hi, multiple data sources se latest data fetch karke list recalculate karne me time lagta hai jisse
   high-traffic me bahut saari requests latest data calculate hone ka wait karne lagengi, aur response delay ho jaayega (user experience kharab hoga). 
   Lekin agar Trending List kuch seconds/minutes purani ho, to generally acceptable hai. Aise cases me Stale-While-Revalidate wala approach useful hai.







==============================================
# QUESTION: CACHE PENETRATION KYA HOTA HAI?
==============================================

Cache Penetration ek aisi situation hai jahan requests repeatedly aise data ke liye aati hain jo Database me exist hi nahi karta. 
Kyunki requested data DB me exist nahi karta, Application usse Cache me store nahi kar sakti — isliye har aisi request par Cache
Miss hota hai aur DB hit hota hai.

Example ke liye, GET /products/999999 — ye product DB me exist nahi karta, Cache me bhi nahi milega. 
Har request pe Cache Miss hoga, phir DB query hogi, aur Not Found return hoga.

Matlab — jo data exist hi nahi karta, uske liye bhi har request DB ko hit kar rahi hai → Database par unnecessary load.



-----------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: CACHE PENETRATION KE KYA SOLUTIONS HAI?
-----------------------------------------------------

Cache Penetration ko prevent karne ke liye primarily
do common approaches use ki ja sakti hain:

1. Negative Caching / Cache Null Value
2. Bloom Filter


1. NEGATIVE CACHING
---------------------

Agar requested data Database me exist nahi karta, to us NOT_FOUND result
ko bhi temporarily Cache me store kar do.

Request → Redis (Cache Miss) → DB → Not Found
                                      ↓
                          Store NOT_FOUND in Redis (TTL = 5 min)
                                      ↓
                                  Return 404

Subsequent requests me, same non-existent product ke liye dobara request aayi to Negative Cache Hit hoga 
aur NOT_FOUND return hoga aur DB query nahi hogi. Isse DB load reduce hota hai.


### QUESTION: TTL KYUN ZAROORI HAI ?
--------------------------------------

1: Agar future me wo data Database me create ho jaaye — lekin Cache invalidation fail ho gaya — to TTL expire hone ke baad 
   next request DB se fresh data fetch karke Cache update kar degi. TTL ke bina, NOT_FOUND entry indefinitely Cache me reh 
   sakti hai — matlab actual data exist hone ke baad bhi user ko NOT_FOUND milta rahega.

2: Bahut saari NOT_FOUND entries Redis memory unnecessarily occupy karengi. TTL se auto-cleanup ho jaata hai.


## QUESTION: NEGATIVE CACHING KI LIMITATION KYA HAI ?
------------------------------------------------------

Negative Caching tab effective hai jab same non-existent ID repeatedly request ki ja rahi ho.
Lekin agar continuously different non-existent IDs ke liye requests aa rahi hain, to har new ID 
ki first request DB tak jaayegi — Negative Caching alone sufficient nahi hoti. Aise cases me 
Bloom Filter use kiya ja sakta hai.




## 2 : BLOOM FILTER
---------------------

- Bloom Filter ek probabilistic data structure hai jo quickly check karta hai ki koi item 
  definitely exist nahi karta ya possibly exist kar sakta hai.

- Ye sirf do tarah ke answers deta hai:

  a) Definitely NOT Present → 100% sure ki item exist nahi karta.
  b) Possibly Present → Item exist kar sakta hai, lekin 100% guarantee nahi hoti. Is case me cache ya Database se further verify karna padta hai.



### QUESTION: BLOOM FILTER INTERNALLY KAISE KAAM KARTA HAI?
-------------------------------------------------------------

- High level par Bloom Filter mainly do cheezon ka use karta hai:
    a) Bit Array
    b) Multiple Hash Functions

- Jab koi item Bloom Filter me add kiya jaata hai, us item ko multiple hash functions me pass kiya jaata hai.
  Ye hash functions multiple positions calculate karte hain, aur un positions ke bits ko Bit Array me 1 set 
  kar diya jaata hai.

- Check karte waqt item ko multiple hash functions ke through pass kiya jaata hai, jo multiple positions calculate karte hain. 
  Phir un positions ke bits check kiye jaate hain bit array me —

    a) At least one required bit = 0 → Definitely NOT Present
    b) All required bits = 1 → Possibly Present

  Lekin yahan ek catch hai — different items ki hash positions kabhi-kabhi overlap kar sakti hain. Is wajah se kisi aise item
  ki bhi saari positions 1 mil sakti hain jo actually Bloom Filter me kabhi add hi nahi hua tha. Isi situation ko False Positive kehte hain.

  Isi wajah se:

     Possibly Present  ≠  Definitely Present



### QUESTION: APPLICATION ME BLOOM FILTER KA USE KAISE HOTA HAI?
------------------------------------------------------------------

Request ko Cache / DB pe bhejne se pehle Bloom Filter check karo:


CASE 1 : DEFINITELY NOT PRESENT
---------------------------------

GET /products/999999
   ↓
Bloom Filter → Definitely NOT Present
   ↓
Return 404 → Cache aur DB hit karne ki zaroorat nahi.


CASE 2 : POSSIBLY PRESENT
----------------------------

GET /products/101
   ↓
Bloom Filter → Possibly Present
   ↓
Redis → Cache Hit?
   ├── YES → Return Data
   └── NO  → Database → Data Found?
                          ├── YES → Store in Redis → Return
                          └── NO  → Return 404




### QUESTION: BLOOM FILTER KA MAIN PURPOSE KYA HAI?
-----------------------------------------------------

Bloom Filter ka main purpose hai invalid ya non-existing requests ko Redis aur Database tak pahunchne se rokna.
Kyunki agar item exist hi nahi karta, to use Redis ya DB tak bhejna unnecessary hai . Isliye Bloom Filter pehle
hi filter kar deta hai ki request aage jaaye ya nahi. Isse Redis load aur Database load dono reduce ho jaate hain.




===========================================
# QUESTION: CACHE AVALANCHE KYA HOTA HAI?
===========================================

- Cache Avalanche ek aisi situation hai jahan bahut saari cache entries ek saath (ya approximately same time par) expire ya unavailable ho jaati hain.
  Is wajah se different cache keys ke liye aane wali requests Cache Miss encounter karti hain aur data fetch karne ke liye Database tak pahunch jaati hain
  jisse Database par sudden load spike aa jaata hai.

- Aisi situation tab aati hai jab bahut saari cache entries similar TTL ke saath, approximately same time par Cache me store ki gayi hon. Kyunki TTL similar hai,
  to wo saari entries bhi approximately same time par expire ho jaati hain. Ab jab un different keys ke liye requests aati hain, to saari requests Cache Miss 
  encounter karti hain aur data fetch karne ke liye Database tak pahunch jaati hain — jisse Database par sudden load spike aa jaata hai.


-------------------------------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: CACHE AVALANCHE KO KAISE SOLVE KARENGE? - TTL JITTER
-------------------------------------------------------------------

- Cache Avalanche tab hota hai jab bahut saari cache entries ek hi ya similar time par expire ho jaati hain. 
  Isse ek saath bahut zyada Cache Misses hoti hain aur Database par sudden load spike aa jaata hai. 
  Solution hai — expiration ko different times me spread out karna.

- Core idea ye hai — jab bhi nayi cache entry create ya update hoti hai, uske Base TTL ke saath thoda random variation
  add kar diya jaata hai. Isse same time ya vicinity me create hui cache entries different times par expire hoti hain 
  jisse cache misses spread out ho jaate hain, database requests spread out ho jaati hain, aur database par sudden load
  spike ka risk reduce ho jaata hai.

- Exmaple: 

    Base TTL = 10 minutes
    Jitter   = Random(0–2 minutes)
    Actual TTL = Base TTL + Random Jitter

    product:101 → TTL = 10 min + 43 sec
    product:102 → TTL = 10 min + 1 min 27 sec
    product:103 → TTL = 10 min + 18 sec



------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: TTL JITTER KAB USEFUL HAI ?
------------------------------------------

Jab bahut saari cache entries similar time window me populate ho rahi hon jaise:

- Application startup / cache warm-up
- Bulk caching

Aise cases me entries ka expiration time synchronize ho sakta hai — aur TTL Jitter usse spread out kar deta hai.




