## QUESTION: HOT KEY KAB BAN SAKTI HAI?
-----------------------------------------

1. Viral Product — Flash sale ke dauran most users same product access karte hain, jisse us product ki cache key Hot Key ban jaati hai.
2. Viral Content — Millions of users ek hi post/content access karte hain, to us content ki cache key Hot Key ban jaati hai.
3. Live Event — Live match ya event ke dauran bahut saare users repeatedly same data (live score, event updates) access karte hain, jisse wo cache key Hot Key ban jaati hai.



--------------------------------------------
## QUESTION: HOT KEY KE SOLUTIONS KYA HAI?
--------------------------------------------

1. LOCAL / IN-MEMORY CACHE
---------------------------

Local Cache (ya In-Memory Cache) ek aisa cache hota hai jo directly Application Instance ki RAM me maintain hota hai. 
Distributed system me multiple Application Instances hote hain, aur har instance apni separate Local Cache maintain 
karta hai — ye Local Caches ek doosre ke saath shared nahi hoti.


### QUESTION: LOCAL CACHE KE SAATH REQUEST FLOW KYA HOTA HAI?
---------------------------------------------------------------

Jab request Application ke paas aati hai, to Application sabse pehle apni Local Cache check karti hai.

- Agar data Local Cache me mil jaata hai (Local Cache Hit), to data directly wahan se return kar diya jaata hai
  Redis tak request hi nahi jaati.

- Agar Local Cache me data nahi milta (Local Cache Miss), to request Redis (Distributed Cache) tak jaati hai.

- Agar Redis me data mil jaata hai (Redis Cache Hit), to data Application ko return hota hai, aur Application usse 
  apni Local Cache me populate kar deti hai — taaki next request Local Cache se serve ho sake.

- Agar Redis me bhi data nahi milta (Redis Cache Miss), to Application Database se data fetch karti hai, usse Redis 
  me store karti hai, apni Local Cache me bhi populate karti hai, aur phir response return karti hai.



### QUESTION: LOCAL CACHE KA BENEFIT KYA HAI?
----------------------------------------------

- Local Cache ka main fayda ye hai ki ye Redis traffic drastically kam karta hai — kyunki frequently accessed data Application Instance 
  ki apni RAM se serve ho jaata hai, isliye Redis tak network call bhi bachti hai aur overall response latency kam hoti hai.

- Ye especially tab useful hota hai jab koi ek key (Hot Key) pe disproportionately high traffic aa rahi ho. Us Hot Key ka data har Application
  Instance ki Local Cache me store ho jaata hai — isliye saari traffic Redis tak pahunchti hi nahi, balki Local Cache se hi serve ho jaati hai.
  Isse us overloaded Redis node pe load drastically kam ho jaata hai.



### QUESTION: LOCAL CACHE KI LIMITATION KYA HAI?
-------------------------------------------------

- Distributed System me multiple Application Instances hote hain, aur har Application Instance apni separate/independent Local Cache maintain karta hai.
  In sabhi instances ke liye ek shared Distributed Cache (jaise Redis) hota hai. 

- Limitation ye hai ki Local Cache invalidation across instances synchronized nahi hoti. Jab koi Instance data ko Database me update karta hai, to wo 
  Redis (Distributed Cache) ko invalidate karta hai, aur apni Local Cache ko bhi invalidate karta hai — lekin doosri instances ki Local Cache ko nahi.
  Kyunki har instance ki Local Cache independent hoti hai, ek instance ki invalidation doosri instances tak automatically propagate nahi hoti.

- Example: Instance 1 ne update kiya → apni Local Cache clear ki. Lekin Instance 2 aur Instance 3 ki Local Cache me purana data abhi bhi stored hai. 
  Agar subsequent request un instances ke paas jaati hai, to stale data return ho jata hai.
 


### QUESTION: LOCAL CACHE KI LIMITATION KO KAISE HANDLE KARTE HAIN?
--------------------------------------------------------------------

- EVENT-BASED CACHE INVALIDATION + SHORT TTL (Safety Net) — dono ko combine karke handle karte hain.

- Jab koi Application Instance data update karti hai (Database me), to wo pehle Redis ko invalidate karti hai, phir apni Local Cache ko, aur uske baad 
  ek Cache Invalidation Event publish karti hai (message broker ke through) — jisme key hoti hai (jaise product:101). Ye event saare instances receive
  karte hain aur apni-apni Local Cache se wo key invalidate kar dete hain.

- Ab next request — chahe kisi bhi instance pe aaye — sabse pehle apni Local Cache check karegi. Kyunki event ke through us key ki entry invalidate ho chuki thi,
  Local Cache Miss hoga. Ab request Redis tak jaati hai — lekin Redis bhi update ke waqt invalidate ho chuka tha, isliye wahan bhi Redis Cache Miss hoga. 
  Ab Application Database se fresh data fetch karti hai, usse Redis me populate karti hai (future requests ke liye), aur apni Local Cache me bhi populate karti hai 
  (is instance pe aane wali next requests ke liye) — phir response client ko return kar deti hai.

- Note: Baaki instances ki Local Cache me us key ki koi entry nahi hai (empty hai). Ab agar us data ke liye request kisi doosre instance pe jaati hai, to wahan bhi
  Local Cache Miss hoga → wo instance Redis se data fetch karega (jo ab populated hai) → apni Local Cache me populate karega → response return karega.

- Short TTL (Safety Net): Lekin possible hai ki kisi instance ko event receive na ho (network issue, instance down, etc.). Aise case me stale data indefinitely na rahe 
  isliye Local Cache entries pe short TTL (jaise 30 sec) configure karte hain. Agar event miss ho jaye, to TTL expire hone pe entry automatically remove ho jaati hai, 
  aur next request pe Redis se fresh data fetch hoga. TTL yaha safety net ka kaam karta hai.





##2. REPLICA-BASED READ DISTRIBUTION
---------------------------------------

- Jab hum Redis ko horizontally scale karte hain, to humare paas ek Redis Cluster hota hai jisme multiple nodes hote hain.
  Har node ek Primary Node hota hai, aur har Primary Node ka apna ek Replica hota hai. Do configurations possible hain:

    a) Default configuration: Primary Node read aur write dono handle karta hai apne set of data ke liye, aur Replica sirf HA ke liye hota hai
       yaani agar Primary Node fail ho jaye, to Replica promote ho jaata hai.

    b) Doosri configuration (Read Replicas): Hum ye bhi configure kar sakte hain ki Primary Node sirf writes handle kare apne data ke liye, aur uske Replicas
       reads handle karein us data ke liye. Ye Redis Client ki configuration se control hota hai — ReadFrom.REPLICA ya ReadFrom.MASTER_PREFERRED.

- Ab maan lo kisi key ke liye bahut zyada read requests aa rahi hain — is wajah se wo Hot Key ban gayi hai. Aise case me us key ke corresponding Primary Node
  ko hum sirf us key ke write operations ke liye reserve kar dete hain, aur read traffic ko Primary Node ke multiple Replicas me distribute kar dete hain jisse
  Primary Node pe load drastically kam ho jaata hai.

        Primary Node 1 (product:101) → sirf writes
        │
        ├── Replica 1 → reads (1/3 traffic)
        ├── Replica 2 → reads (1/3 traffic)
        └── Replica 3 → reads (1/3 traffic)

   Read load 3 replicas me distribute → Primary Node free for writes

- Ye configuration read-heavy Hot Key ke liye effective hai. Agar Hot Key pe writes zyada hain (jaise Rate Limiter ka counter INCR), 
  to Read Replicas help nahi karenge — kyunki writes Primary Node pe hi jaayenge. Us case me Key Salting zyada effective hai.


### QUESTION: REPLICA-BASED READ DISTRIBUTION KI LIMITATION KYA HAI?
----------------------------------------------------------------------

- REPLICATION DELAY — Primary Node pe hone wale writes (yaani key ke corresponding value ko update karna ya key ko delete karna) Redis ke internal replication mechanism 
  ke through Replicas tak propagate hote hain. Ye propagation instant nahi hoti — Primary aur Replicas ke beech thoda delay hota hai.
      
      Primary  → Price = ₹120  (updated)
      Replica 1 → Price = ₹120  (synced)
      Replica 2 → Price = ₹100  (abhi sync nahi hua)

  Agar isi time read request Replica 2 pe route ho jaye, to temporarily stale data return ho sakta hai.

- Read traffic multiple nodes me distribute hota hai, lekin replication delay ki wajah se temporary stale reads possible hain.


     

